lder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"computer_name": {"description": "The folder's computer_name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}, "environment_id": {"description": "The folder's environment_id from list_computer_folders.", "type": "string"}, "folder": {"description": "The folder's name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["environment_id", "computer_name", "folder"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description cal